1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,100
On the first of June, 1946, the most decorated soldier in the Soviet Union walked into a meeting room in Moscow and found himself on trial. No judge. No jury.

2
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:25,900
Just a long table of marshals and generals, and near the head of it, a short man in a plain grey tunic, smoking a pipe. The charges came from a dossier prepared by the secret police. The accused was arrogant.

3
00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:40,300
Disloyal. He had taken credit for victories that belonged to others. One of his friends, the marshal in charge of the air force, had already been arrested and kept awake for days until he signed a statement against him.

4
00:00:40,300 --> 00:00:50,300
The word that hung over the room was one every Soviet officer dreaded. Bonapartism. The suspicion that a general had grown popular enough to become a Napoleon.

5
00:00:50,300 --> 00:01:05,900
Here is what makes that scene so strange. Less than a year earlier, this same man had ridden a white horse across Red Square to lead the Victory Parade, while the man with the pipe watched from the top of Lenin's tomb.

6
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:16,300
He was the general who had held Moscow, trapped an entire German army at Stalingrad, and taken Berlin. And here is the part that should stop you cold.

7
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:33,300
None of that saved him. It was the reason he was in the room. Within days he was stripped of his command and sent south to run the Odessa military district, a posting so far from power it was practically an insult with a uniform attached.

8
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:43,300
Agents searched his homes, building a case that could have ended with a bullet. He had survived Hitler's army. Now he had to survive his own government.

9
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:57,400
His name was Georgy Zhukov. A peasant's son, apprenticed to a furrier at eleven, who became the one man Stalin could never bring himself to trust. So how does a furrier's boy become the general a dictator fears?

10
00:01:57,400 --> 00:02:08,100
Let's break down Georgy Zhukov's three greatest tactical moves of the Second World War. Before the battles, one detail explains almost everything. Zhukov was not trained to be cautious.

11
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:19,200
He was trained to be hard. As a young cavalryman in the First World War, he won two St. George Crosses and was left half deaf by a mine blast.

12
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:34,800
In the Red Army of the 1930s, he was blunt, stubborn, and feared for his temper. And he absorbed a revolutionary Soviet idea called deep operations. Do not simply push the enemy back. Break through, swing around, and destroy him from behind.

13
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:47,800
The first one happened in a war most people have never heard of. Summer, 1939. Eastern Mongolia. An empty sea of grass on the border between Soviet-backed Mongolia and the Japanese puppet state of Manchukuo.

14
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:58,500
Through it ran a slow river called the Khalkhin Gol. The Japanese insisted the border sat on the river. The Mongolians said it sat about 20 km further east.

15
00:02:58,500 --> 00:03:10,700
By June, the shooting over that strip of grass had become a real war that neither Moscow nor Tokyo had ever declared. Zhukov arrived as a 42-year-old corps commander carrying a quiet fear.

16
00:03:10,700 --> 00:03:25,500
Stalin's purges had just shot or imprisoned thousands of Red Army officers, including three of the five marshals. Mongolia was a test, and he knew exactly what happened to men who failed tests. The Japanese made one deeply comfortable assumption.

17
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:39,900
The nearest Soviet railhead was roughly 650 km away across open steppe. No proper roads. Almost no water. Every shell and every loaf of bread had to come by truck, and a single round trip could take five days.

18
00:03:39,900 --> 00:03:51,800
Any large Soviet offensive would starve before it started. And if the Soviets did build up, the Japanese would see it coming, because you cannot hide an army on a billiard table.

19
00:03:51,800 --> 00:04:02,200
Zhukov looked at the same empty plain and saw a stage. If the Japanese could see everything, then he would decide exactly what they saw. First, the trucks.

20
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:18,500
More than 4,000 of them, running around the clock, mostly at night, until roughly 57,000 men and close to 500 tanks were in position. Then, the performance. His radio operators sent messages in codes the Japanese could break, all about digging in for winter.

21
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:28,900
A pamphlet on how to fight a defensive war was printed and allowed to drift into Japanese hands. Loudspeakers played the sound of pile drivers night after night.

22
00:04:28,900 --> 00:04:41,500
And for nearly two weeks, tanks drove up and down the line in the dark with their mufflers stripped off, roaring for hours and then going nowhere at all. Think about what that did.

23
00:04:41,500 --> 00:04:53,000
By August, the Japanese had stopped listening. The roar of engines at night had become as ordinary as the wind. At dawn on August 20th, a Sunday, the roar was real.

24
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:06,700
Artillery and bombers hit the Japanese centre while armoured columns swung wide around both flanks. Within days the pincers met behind the Japanese 23rd Division. By the end of the month, the pocket had been wiped out.

25
00:05:06,700 --> 00:05:20,000
Let us be honest, this victory was not cheap. Japanese records count roughly 8,000 dead. Soviet archives admit to around 9,700 killed or missing. Zhukov paid more lives than he took. That would become a pattern.

26
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:34,100
But measure it by what it bought. Japan gave up on a war northward into Siberia. In April 1941 it signed a neutrality pact with the Soviet Union and turned its ambitions south, toward a harbour in Hawaii.

27
00:05:34,100 --> 00:05:47,400
That meant the great Soviet armies guarding the Far East could one day be moved. Remember those divisions. Two years later, they would arrive at the gates of Moscow. The lesson of Khalkhin Gol is simple.

28
00:05:47,400 --> 00:05:58,900
If the enemy can see everything, give him something to look at. If stories like this are why you're here, hitting subscribe takes a second and helps more than you'd think.

29
00:05:58,900 --> 00:06:11,500
Because the next time Zhukov was needed, he was fighting for the capital itself. October 1941. Hitler launched Operation Typhoon, the final drive on Moscow. Near Vyazma and Bryansk, entire Soviet armies were encircled.

30
00:06:11,500 --> 00:06:23,700
Germany claimed more than 600,000 prisoners. On October 10th, Stalin handed what was left of the Western Front to Zhukov. In Moscow, government offices were evacuated east and factories were wired with explosives.

31
00:06:23,700 --> 00:06:41,100
By late November, German forward units were roughly 30 km from the Kremlin. And the German high command was sure of one thing. The Red Army was spent. Their intelligence officers had counted every Soviet division destroyed since June and concluded there could not be much left.

32
00:06:41,100 --> 00:06:53,000
One more push, and the city would fall before the snow got deep. Zhukov's insight was the most dangerous kind, because it demanded that he do almost nothing with his best troops.

33
00:06:53,000 --> 00:07:01,100
Throwing every fresh division into the line the moment it arrived would only feed the German machine one meal at a time.

34
00:07:01,100 --> 00:07:19,200
Moscow would be saved by holding with the bare minimum, letting the Germans exhaust themselves, and keeping a hammer hidden behind the city. Picture what that meant. Ragged rifle divisions and militia units of factory workers and students holding the line, while fresh armies sat waiting to the rear.

35
00:07:19,200 --> 00:07:29,200
Some of them were the divisions from Siberia and the Far East. A Soviet spy in Tokyo had reported that Japan would not attack in the east.

36
00:07:29,200 --> 00:07:41,800
Khalkhin Gol had already taught Japan that lesson, and now the dividend was paid. That November, Stalin called and asked Zhukov whether he was sure they would hold Moscow. Answer honestly, as a communist.

37
00:07:41,800 --> 00:07:54,800
Zhukov did not flatter him. They would hold it, he said, but he needed two more armies and at least 200 tanks. He got the armies. The tanks were another matter. Then the weather turned.

38
00:07:54,800 --> 00:08:05,900
Temperatures fell far below minus 30. German engines would not start. Men in summer uniforms lost fingers and toes. Typhoon blew itself out on the frozen edges of the city.

39
00:08:05,900 --> 00:08:20,700
On December 5th and 6th, 1941, the hammer came down. Soviet armies the Germans never knew existed struck along a front hundreds of kilometres long. White-clad ski troops. Cavalry in snowbound forests. Tanks with wide tracks riding over the drifts.

40
00:08:20,700 --> 00:08:33,700
If you watched our episode on Heinz Guderian, this is the counterattack that sent his frozen panzers reeling and cost him his command. Within weeks, the Germans were driven back between 100 and 250 km.

41
00:08:33,700 --> 00:08:45,200
For the first time in the war, the German army had suffered a strategic defeat on land. Hitler sacked the commander in chief of his army and took the job himself.

42
00:08:45,200 --> 00:09:00,800
The cost in Soviet lives was terrible. But two months earlier, the Soviet state had been packing its bags. The lesson of Moscow cuts against every instinct. The hardest discipline in war is holding your reserve while everything around you is burning.

43
00:09:00,800 --> 00:09:10,400
September 1942. The German 6th Army, under General Friedrich Paulus, was fighting its way into Stalingrad street by street, house by house, sometimes room by room.

44
00:09:10,400 --> 00:09:26,300
The Soviet defenders clung to a strip of the Volga riverbank, in places only a few hundred metres deep. In Stalin's office, Zhukov and the chief of staff, Aleksandr Vasilevsky, murmured to each other that they needed, as they put it, another solution.

45
00:09:26,300 --> 00:09:35,600
Stalin overheard, and sent them away to work it out. The German assumption was this. The Soviets were pouring everything they had into the city.

46
00:09:35,600 --> 00:09:49,700
So the Germans, too, fed their best units into the ruins, and left the long, open flanks to their Romanian allies. Brave men, but thinly spread, short of anti-tank guns, and with almost no armour of their own.

47
00:09:49,700 --> 00:10:02,300
Zhukov and Vasilevsky looked at the burning city and saw bait. Stalingrad did not need to win. It only needed to hold, just barely, and keep the 6th Army's attention locked inside the rubble.

48
00:10:02,300 --> 00:10:14,900
Over two months, more than a million Soviet troops, nearly 900 tanks, and some 13,000 guns moved onto the flanks in near silence. They moved by night. Radio traffic was cut to almost nothing.

49
00:10:14,900 --> 00:10:29,000
Inside the city, the defenders were starved of reinforcements, because the starving was part of the plan. Commanders gave orders in person instead of by signal. Civilians in front-line villages were moved out so no one could talk.

50
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:40,100
On November 19th, in freezing fog, thousands of Soviet guns opened fire on the Romanian line north of the city. The next day, a second blow fell to the south.

51
00:10:40,100 --> 00:10:56,400
Soviet tanks poured through, not into the city, but around it. On November 23rd, the pincers met near Kalach on the Don. The ring was closed. Now let that sink in. The German army had spent three months fighting its way to the Volga.

52
00:10:56,400 --> 00:11:04,900
Zhukov's trap needed four days to close behind it. Between 250,000 and 300,000 Axis soldiers were inside. Hitler forbade them to break out.

53
00:11:04,900 --> 00:11:19,300
A relief attack failed, and the promised airlift delivered only a fraction of what they needed to stay alive. On January 31st, 1943, Paulus surrendered. Around 91,000 men marched into captivity. Only about 5,000 would ever see Germany again.

54
00:11:19,300 --> 00:11:29,300
The lesson of Stalingrad is the coldest of the three. Do not try to win the battle your enemy has chosen. Win the one behind his back.

55
00:11:29,300 --> 00:11:46,300
Two years later, Zhukov's armies took Berlin, and he sat at the head of the table as Germany signed its final surrender. Then came the white horse, and then the room where we began. To Stalin, a famous general with loyal officers was not a treasure.

56
00:11:46,300 --> 00:11:56,300
He was a threat. Eisenhower, who met him in Berlin, became something close to a friend and invited him to America. Stalin made sure he never went.

57
00:11:56,300 --> 00:12:12,600
Instead, Zhukov went to Odessa, then to the Urals, and waited, knowing exactly what had happened to men like him in 1937. Stalin died in 1953, and Zhukov came back. He helped arrest Stalin's secret police chief, Lavrentiy Beria, and became minister of defence.

58
00:12:12,600 --> 00:12:23,700
In 1957, his support helped keep Nikita Khrushchev in power. A few months later, Khrushchev fired him too, with the very same accusation. Bonapartism. Two leaders. Two dismissals. One fear.

59
00:12:23,700 --> 00:12:35,600
So let's lay the three moves side by side. At Khalkhin Gol, he trained his enemy to stop listening. At Moscow, he held his hammer back until the enemy had nothing left.

60
00:12:35,600 --> 00:12:49,700
At Stalingrad, he turned a ruined city into bait. Three moves, one idea. Make the enemy look at the wrong thing. But a serious history shows both sides of the ledger. Zhukov spent lives with a cold hand.

61
00:12:49,700 --> 00:13:04,100
His offensive at Rzhev, codenamed Mars, was a bloody failure Soviet histories buried for decades. His rush for Berlin cost tens of thousands of lives in days. He threatened his own officers with execution, and sometimes carried it out.

62
00:13:04,100 --> 00:13:18,200
And Vasilevsky deserves far more credit for Stalingrad than the legend allows. He was not a gentle man. But he was the one Soviet commander who stood in every great crisis, from Mongolia to Berlin, and never broke.

63
00:13:18,200 --> 00:13:28,900
Russians often compare him to Mikhail Kutuzov, who also saved Moscow by refusing to spend his army too early, and who was also distrusted by the ruler he saved.

64
00:13:28,900 --> 00:13:40,800
And that is the irony of Georgy Zhukov. Two leaders feared he would reach for power. Yet with armies that would have followed him anywhere, he never once tried to seize it.

65
00:13:40,800 --> 00:13:53,800
The general the dictators feared most was the one who kept saving them. So, which of Zhukov's three moves impressed you most? And whose story should we take apart next? Leave it in the comments.
